 For remote sign-in, use **Device code** for Codex. Codex's **In browser** mode needs a browser on the daemon computer because its callback uses localhost. Claude's manual authorization-code flow can be completed from another device.
 
+## Configured Codex pools
+
+Use a pool for existing `extends: "codex"` providers whose original commands already select distinct accounts. Build this checkout and reload the plugin first; it writes `$PASEO_HOME/account-switcher/bin/codex-pool-launcher`. Enable Codex automatic failover in the account registry (`autoFailover.codex`). Pool selection does not enable paid credits, prepaid quota resets, or another model.
+
+For each provider, preserve its original command in the JSON value of `env.PASEO_CODEX_ACCOUNT_POOL`, then point its command at the pool launcher. Paths below are examples; use absolute paths on your machine:
+
+```json
+{
+  "extends": "codex",
+  "label": "Codex work",
+  "command": [
+    "/home/me/.paseo/account-switcher/bin/codex-pool-launcher",
+    "--root",
+    "/home/me/.paseo/account-switcher",
+    "--provider",
+    "codex-work",
+    "--config",
+    "/home/me/.paseo/config.json",
+    "--"
+  ],
+  "env": {
+    "CODEX_HOME": "/home/me/.codex-work",
+    "PASEO_CODEX_ACCOUNT_POOL": "{\"pool\":\"personal\",\"command\":[\"/usr/local/bin/codex\"],\"home\":\"/home/me/.codex-work\"}"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+The key under `agents.providers` must match `--provider`. Account names and pool names are arbitrary. Keep the provider's other environment overrides. Run `paseo reload` to apply provider command changes without restarting the daemon. Existing processes finish their current turn; a quota recovery reopens only the affected agent.
+
+For a remote account, `command` inside the metadata is the existing local remote-launch script. Add `ssh` with its configured SSH host alias and set `home` to the absolute Codex home **on that host**. Python 3.9+ and noninteractive SSH must work on that host. The original command must execute workspace tools on the same development environment as before; the pool does not synchronize workspaces or reconfigure tunnels.
+
+Opting in trusts the selected hosts with that conversation. Transfer is restricted to the native thread's rollout and its recorded child threads; auth files, configuration, databases, memories, and unrelated sessions are excluded. The old process closes before the launcher copies history. Transfer streams 64 KiB chunks, with limits of 512 MiB per rollout, 1 GiB per conversation, and 256 recorded threads. A 120-second transfer deadline bounds unavailable or stalled hosts. A missing source, an escaping symlink, or divergent destination history stops the switch. The plugin does not replace the conversation with an empty thread.
+
+Set `enabled: false` **inside the metadata JSON** to remove an account from future selection while retaining the provider adapter needed by its existing tabs. The next selection rereads membership; no plugin reload is needed for this edit. Keep the original provider entry while tabs still belong to it, even after those tabs switch accounts: Paseo's native persistence handle still names that provider. Delete the provider only after those tabs are no longer needed. Do not delete a source history directory while a recovery still needs it.
+
+Recovery intentions and account bindings survive plugin reload. A missing or no-longer-visible agent is retired from the queue; transient RPC errors remain retryable. Local TCP (IPv4/IPv6) and Unix-socket daemons wake the recovery queue through the plugin's own RPC. Recovery uses a 180-second local reload budget; a lost acknowledgement is reconciled before another account is attempted. Turning off `autoFailover.codex` stops further selection and continuation. Native quota/model checks share at most two concurrent probe processes across recovering chats; reopening a removed assignment probes at most two candidates at a time in that launcher.
+
+In a client with model-menu account support, open the model picker and select its account title. The current account follows the original thread's confirmed launch receipt. The original configuration still identifies the provider and its saved profiles. Select an account to move an idle conversation. During a turn, the choice waits until the daemon can reserve the idle session. You can cancel a queued choice. An accepted manual switch leaves a paused chat paused. Older daemons can show the current account and need an update for manual switching.
+
+Use **Pause automatic continuation** in the checking, waiting, or switching row to pause one conversation. Your next message can resume it. A reload already submitted can finish opening the selected account after the pause has been saved.
+
+Malformed pool entries are skipped individually. If a transferred conversation's target is removed or disabled, its next launch selects an eligible account and transfers from its last confirmed history location. Keep the original provider adapter enabled as described above. If no account can reopen it yet, the launcher leaves a durable recovery request; the plugin picks it up even when startup emits no failed-turn event. The workspace and any external services must still be reachable through the selected account's configured command.
+
+Automatic quota recovery uses the legacy reload RPC and checks ownership just before sending it. A user prompt submitted at that exact boundary can still be interrupted. Manual menu choices require the daemon's guarded idle reload.
+
 ## Update
 
 Finish or cancel active sign-ins first: reloading the plugin stops its background sign-in processes.
